WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:05.200
The newest adaptation of Stephen King's It
took a few creative liberties with the story

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.960
setting and events, so it's probably no surprise
that some of the characters described in the

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.510
1986 novel don't come through quite same as
they'd been written.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:18.250
Although the Losers and their nemeses certainly
carry the spirit of the source material through

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:23.939
to the big screen, let's take a look at how
they stack up to the book's original descriptions.

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:25.710
Bill Denbrough

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:30.360
Jaeden Lieberher was previously seen in Midnight
Special and Showtime's Masters of Sex before

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.710
becoming Bill Denbrough for It.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.829
Bill — also known as Stuttering Bill — is
the de facto leader of the Losers' Club and

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:40.710
is described as having a "goofball grin" and
anxious blue eyes.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.960
He's also the tallest and most authoritative
member of the group.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.289
After the group's childhood years, he'd later
be compared to a teenage JFK for his reddish

00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:49.289
hair.

00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:52.670
Onscreen, Lieberher looks quite different
than the Bill described in the book.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:57.030
He's not particularly taller or more imposing
than the others, but he still makes it clear

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:01.600
he's the one who's lost the most — and has
the most at stake down in the sewers.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.360
He doesn't have book-Bill's shock of red hair,
either.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.270
Considering he manages to pull off Bill's
passion for the Pennywise chase, however,

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:09.940
it still works.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.400
Richie Tozier

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.520
Stranger Things star Finn Wolfhard doubled
down on his '80s horror appeal by starring

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.520
as Richie Tozier, the scrawny, bespectacled
kid who makes up what he lacks in size with

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:21.520
zingers.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.320
In the book, Richie is described as having
such oversized teeth that he earns the nickname

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:29.720
"Bucky Beaver," and has lenses so thick that
he'd be blind without them.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:34.400
Those glasses are also constantly being broken,
dropped, kicked, and bent in the book, and

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.030
they're almost always lopsided and mended
with tape.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:40.690
And his mouth constantly gets him into trouble,
which is what earns him the childhood nickname

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.120
"Trashmouth."

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.310
Since most of the book's physical descriptions
of Richie revolve around his glasses and chompers,

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.520
Wolfhard is halfway there to perfection.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:54.870
He might not have those prominent teeth, but
his specs and sass are definitely on point.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.340
Ben Hanscom

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.600
Relative newcomer Jeremy Ray Taylor may be
familiar to fans of the Marvel Universe thanks

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.590
to his role as a bully in Ant-Man.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:07.290
In It, however, he made a change of pace by
playing Ben Hanscom, the boy who's actually

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.830
on the receiving end of so many taunts.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:13.890
Ben is described as wearing very loose-fitting
clothing because of the constant ridicule

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.920
he receives for his weight.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:21.120
Inside, he's a very sweet, intelligent, and
dependable dude, but all anyone can see when

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.970
they look at him is his size.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:28.560
He's estimated to weigh around 150 to 160
pounds and seems quite insecure about his

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.560
appearance.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.810
In the role, Taylor pulls off not just the
physical attributes of Ben, but the emotional

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.370
presence of the character as well.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.980
Beverly Marsh

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:41.650
Sophia Lillis is a relative newcomer to the
movie business, but fans of It won't soon

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:42.890
forget her name.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:46.750
Starring as Beverly Marsh, the lone lady of
the Loser's Club, Lillis manages to bring

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:50.680
forth some of the subtler qualities of the
character that hadn't quite been seen in the

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.290
1990 TV mini-series adaptation.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.410
In Stephen King's original version, Beverly
is described as being less financially fortunate

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:01.100
than the others — a fact that comes through
in her wardrobe, with her thrift-store clothes

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.660
and scuffed shoes.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.620
From the boys' perspective, she's described
as being very pretty, with hair that is long

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.350
and auburn, eyes that are grayish green, and
a pale complexion.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:15.350
She's also got a smattering freckles and a
smile that is seen as "wise, cynical, and

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.380
sad all at the same time."

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.260
For all those reasons, Lillis' casting is
essentially perfect.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.040
Although her long hair does look a little
false in the beginning of the movie, she still

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.319
pulls off the look with convincing poise.

00:03:26.319 --> 00:03:30.260
More importantly, she captures that subtle
strength and world-weariness that made the

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:31.920
character such a force in the novel.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.709
"I can take that."

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:35.630
Eddie Kaspbrak

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:39.600
Jack Dylan Grazer capped a brief string of
early supporting roles with the part of Eddie

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:45.110
Kaspbrak, one of Bill's longtime closest friends
and a charter member of the Losers' Club.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:48.060
Most of King's descriptions of Eddie center
around his asthma.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:52.380
Plagued by his helicopter mom, he's small,
slight, and fragile — even as an adult,

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.910
when his face is described as "thin and delicate-looking."

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:59.600
Eddie doesn't get much of a physical description
in the book, so there weren't many ways the

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.360
filmmakers could go wrong.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.580
While Grazer's Eddie looks entirely different
than the 1990s version, he still captures

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:10.020
his character's apparent frailties while still
being a lot stronger than he looks.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.300
Mike Hanlon

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:15.590
Hawaii Five-O vet Chosen Jacobs traded the
tropics for Maine when he took on the role

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:16.829
of It's Mike Hanlon.

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:21.120
A farm kid growing up on the outskirts of
town, Mike is from the only black family in

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.400
Derry and is constantly tormented by Henry
Bowers and his bully crew.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:30.660
As described in the book, Mike has been toughened
up by physically laborious work, and is fast,

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:31.660
agile, and slim.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:33.770
Jacobs is a brilliant casting choice for Mike.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:37.749
Not only does he have the big, tough, farm
kid physicality in play, but he also has an

00:04:37.749 --> 00:04:43.810
emotional vulnerability that makes his narrative
arc so much more interesting to watch unfold.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:44.810
Stan Uris

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:50.320
You might recognize Wyatt Oleff as the young
Star-Lord in the Guardians of the Galaxy movies,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:54.600
but there's no doubt everyone will remember
him now as Stanley Uris from It.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.900
In the book, Stanley is described as having
black hair and a nose that doesn't quite suit

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.490
the stereotype for a person of the Jewish
faith.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:06.130
He's also a little older than the other children
due to being held back a grade as a kid.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:09.870
Oleff's version doesn't have the black hair
that Stan's described to have in the novel.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:16.050
But he does have his fussy mannerisms, neatness,
and sense of shaken faith down to a science.

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:17.750
Henry Bowers

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:22.150
It's secondary antagonist Henry Bowers is
only 12 in the book, but he looks much bigger

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:26.970
to the Losers because of the fact that he's
flunked enough to still be in their same grade.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:30.960
Unlike the film, which moves the action to
the '80s, the book is set in the '50s.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:35.370
And Henry is the embodiment of the prototypical
bully from that era, with a flat top haircut,

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:39.530
a pink motorcycle jacket, and the physical
wear of his father's heavy hand.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:44.740
For the 2017 version, Nicholas Hamilton's
Henry Bowers got a complete wardrobe overhaul

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:49.370
to match the new time period, and instead
of the slick hairstyle, he sports a mullet.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:53.580
The new Henry Bowers is still just as classically
mean as prior versions, though, so that's

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.310
what counts.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:56.930
Patrick Hockstetter

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:01.780
Although Henry Bowers was bad enough, his
sidekick Patrick was really horrible at his

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:02.780
core.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:06.640
Played by Owen Teague from Bloodline and Black
Mirror, Patrick is the student who triggers

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.130
a whole different level of fear in those he
terrorizes with his wandering hands and pencil

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.330
case full of dead flies.

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:16.750
In the novel, he's heavyset with a "slack
and pallid moonface."

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:20.610
One of his teachers even describes him as
"a big lump of clay that had been crudely

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.470
fashioned to look like a boy."

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:27.020
He's also the kind of completely deranged
guy who would murder his infant brother in

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.020
his crib.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.980
Teague, who already has a slew of credits
to his name, looks nothing like the heavyset,

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:32.680
expressionless pre-teen of the book.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:36.400
And in the film version, the essence of the
character has been dialed down dramatically

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.560
to the point that he's really no more than
a schoolyard bully with an aerosol fire gun.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:42.560
Pennywise

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.430
Although he's scary in his own right, Bill
Skarsgård's Pennywise represents a significant

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.040
visual change from the novel.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.400
His new costuming was inspired by Elizabethan
and medieval-era clown and jester costumes

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.770
rather than the baggy silver suit with orange
buttons that's described in King's book.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:02.510
King also describes him as having yellow eyes,
and reminds poor Georgie of Bozo the clown.

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:06.720
He's later compared to Ronald McDonald for
his painted-white face and massive smile,

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.370
and in addition to his long, sharp teeth,
he's also mostly bald, except for an orangish-red

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:13.610
tuft of hair on each side of his head.

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:17.370
The Pennywise of the movie doesn't quite resemble
what King originally imagined — but he's

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:21.949
still the kind of clown you wouldn't want
to run across at a carnival, so it works.

00:07:21.949 --> 00:07:22.949
Thanks for watching!

00:07:22.949 --> 00:07:24.930
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:26.900
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

